-- The language server: `meadow lsp`, MeadowBoot's, declared with Lingua's
-- `lsp!` over what the compiler already works out.
--
-- It answers what the Rust compiler's server answers
-- (`buildtools/meadow-lsp`), in the same shapes: published diagnostics,
-- hover, completion, go to definition, workspace symbols, formatting, inlay
-- hints, code lenses, prepare rename and rename, semantic tokens, the
-- custom `meadow/ir`. `buildtools/meadow/tests/lsp_differential.rs` drives
-- both with the same messages and compares what they answer; where the two
-- still differ, that test says how far.
--
-- Lingua speaks the protocol. Every request is one of the language's own
-- (`requests`), so that each answer is written in the shape the Rust server
-- writes it, and what the server says it can do is the Rust server's
-- (`capabilities`).
--
-- A document belongs to its package, as it does for the Rust server: when
-- one is opened, the package around it is built as `meadow build` would --
-- the units it depends on first, by a run of this program, so that nothing
-- the build prints reaches the editor -- and what each unit was compiled
-- from is read back (`givenUnits`). The package is then compiled again, with
-- the open documents' texts standing for their files, whenever one of them
-- has changed since it last was.

